{"kind":"request","text":"claude: close the advisory citation backlog the newly-direct documentation gate has made visible. Sequenced AFTER your decision-records repair under d14e96fb — that lane stays first, and nothing here is urgent. You found this while reviewing the citation migration and said it was worth its own request; this is that request.\n\nWHAT IT IS, MEASURED ON MERGED MAIN RATHER THAN ESTIMATED. I merged the citation migration at d51ac8f3 and then ran the gate verbosely at that commit, because a passing run hides its own t.Logf output and the count would otherwise be invisible. At main d51ac8f3: 64 advisory citations, 0 fatal, across 27 distinct documentation pages. The advisory class is anchor_test.go line 76 — an artifact retired WITH a successor covering the same path, where the gate says the pages naming it should re-anchor there. The fatal class, line 73, is a withdrawn pointer with no successor, and there are none of those left.\n\nWHY THIS IS WORTH DOING, AND WHY IT IS NOT URGENT. A page resting on a retired-with-successor artifact is not broken. It carries real provenance and the gate deliberately does not fail it — you made exactly this point in your verdict, and it settled a design question I had been getting wrong. But that provenance points at a predecessor rather than at what the successor now says, so the flare that should reach the page when the world moves under it lands on a retired row instead. Twenty-seven pages are one step behind the world they describe. That is documentation drift with a known, mechanical repair, not a defect.\n\nIt is also the debt that the exception list was hiding. Deleting that list is what made these 64 countable at all, so the work of closing them is the second half of the value the migration bought.\n\nWHAT I AM NOT ASKING FOR. Do not re-anchor a page onto an artifact whose commit is not contained in main — that is the mistake that turned the previous citation head red, and every anchor must be checked with git merge-base --is-ancestor per commit rather than by reading SHAs. Do not treat this as a licence to restructure the pages; the change is front matter only unless a page's prose is actually falsified by what the successor says, in which case say so and stop rather than rewriting it inside this lane. And do not chase the count to zero if some anchor has no honest successor to move to: report what could not be moved and why. A truthful 6 remaining beats a tidy 0.\n\nSize it yourself. If 27 pages is too much for one head, split by area and tell me the split you want; I would rather have three reviewable heads than one that nobody can check.","body":{"conditions":"Sequenced after the decision-records repair under d14e96fb. Re-anchor documentation pages carrying advisory citations (anchor_test.go line 76 class, artifact retired with a successor covering the same path) onto their live successors. Baseline measured at main d51ac8f3: 64 advisory citations across 27 pages, 0 fatal. Every new anchor must be a live artifact whose commit is verified contained in main with git merge-base --is-ancestor per commit. Front matter only unless a page's prose is actually falsified, in which case report it rather than rewriting in this lane. Any citation with no honest successor is reported with its reason rather than forced. Zero fatal citations at the delivered head, gate re-run with -count=1 and the advisory count stated from a verbose run. One or more exact clean pushed heads with per-path artifacts and independent review; propose a split if one head would be too large to review. Do not merge.","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}